 \subsection{Care, Professional Judgment, and Relational Systems}
 
-Medical AI illustrates how the principle supports high-value automation. A decision aid may outperform a clinician on a bounded prediction, extend expertise to an underserved setting, or detect a pattern a person would miss. The professional duty can nevertheless require facts outside the model, patient preferences, uncertainty, informed consent, follow-up, and intervention when the workflow fails. A 2026 medical-AI consensus accordingly combines access evaluation and real-world validation with multidisciplinary review, traceability, continuing monitoring, competence, and circuit breakers.\lrfootnote{See \artcite{caoMedicalConsensus2026}.} Recent scholarship identifies four conditions for meaningful oversight---epistemic capacity, cognitive space, decisional authority, and intervention effectiveness---and treats oversight as a property of the clinical system rather than a ceremonial human presence.\lrfootnote{See \artcite{vanDeSandeOversight2026}; see also \bluecite{whoEthicsAIHealth2021}.}
+Medical AI supplies the clearest affirmative case for the anti-substitution principle. The protected function is not that a human manually perform every calculation. It is that professional judgment remain available at the point where law requires a prescribing or care decision: an actor must be able to integrate patient-specific facts, preferences, uncertainty, contraindications, and follow-up needs, including information outside the model, and must possess authority to decline, alter, or stop the proposed course. A system may perform most of the analytic work while preserving that function. A 2026 medical-AI consensus accordingly combines access evaluation and real-world validation with multidisciplinary review, traceability, continuing monitoring, competence, and circuit breakers. Related scholarship identifies epistemic capacity, cognitive space, decisional authority, and intervention effectiveness as conditions of meaningful oversight and treats oversight as a property of the clinical system rather than a ceremonial human presence.\lrfootnote{See \artcite{caoMedicalConsensus2026}; \artcite{vanDeSandeOversight2026}; see also \bluecite{whoEthicsAIHealth2021}.}
 
-Under the four-question rule, the institution first states the actual duty: for example, competent professional judgment and follow-up under the applicable law. It then identifies which functions the model performs and which remain elsewhere in the workflow. The cascade shows whether the clinician receives enough information, time, and authority to act; the charter shows who responds when performance drifts or an exception exceeds the model's scope. AI can carry a large share of the work. Responsibility stays with the professional and institution that law authorizes and regulates.
+A 2026 preprint by Eileanor LaRocco and coauthors gives that functional account an unusually direct clinical measure. In a survey of 136 U.S. prescribing clinicians, the authors proposed three minimum architectural conditions for autonomous prescribing: calibrated per-prediction confidence capable of gating action and escalation; differentiated communication of epistemic uncertainty arising from model ignorance and aleatoric uncertainty arising from genuine clinical ambiguity; and inferential transparency at the moment of decision sufficient to support audit and responsibility allocation.\lrfootnote{See \bluecite{laroccoCliniciansVeto2026}. The study used a survey, not interviews, and recruited clinicians with current U.S. prescribing authority rather than physicians alone. Its instrument separately recorded clinical role and whether prescribing authority was full independent, collaborative, restricted, or limited.}
+
+The results do not establish a standard of care or a rule of legal liability. They identify conditions under which clinicians were willing to occupy the decisionmaking position to which professional responsibility may attach. Baseline support for autonomous prescribing without clinician sign-off was low. Of the respondents, 67.4\% selected the strongest response that they would not permit AI agents to prescribe without calibrated escalation. Respondents preferred a competing-options summary in the scenario representing genuine clinical ambiguity, but shifted significantly toward abstention when the scenario represented model ignorance. Across all four adverse-outcome scenarios, they assigned greater responsibility to organizational actors than to clinicians and patients; the organizational--individual gap narrowed when the system communicated confidence, escalated the case, and the clinician concurred with documented reasons.\lrfootnote{See \bluecite{laroccoCliniciansVeto2026}. The authors characterize the three features as necessary but not sufficient for adoption and note that inferential transparency was tested through a contrast among responsibility-allocation scenarios rather than by a direct preference item.}
+
+The direction matters. The survey does not describe a safety-induced deadlock. It finds that the proposed features can increase acceptance by preserving a clinician's substantive cognitive position---and that preserving it collapses much of what ``autonomy'' conventionally means. The finding should not be reduced to a demand for citations appended to an answer. What the responsibility scenarios test is whether the clinician occupies an epistemic position from which a substantive judgment can be made under acknowledged uncertainty. That is the clinical form of the principle developed here: automation performs professional duty when it preserves the protected judgment, not when it leaves a human name attached to an action the human could not meaningfully assess.
+
+That translation also changes the technical object. The paper states model-level requirements. A deployed prescribing agent may instead be a pipeline of retrieval, tools, submodels, deterministic rules, and reranking. A confidence score calibrated for one component does not automatically express confidence in the final action, and a model-level epistemic--aleatoric classification may not capture missing records, tool failure, or ambiguity introduced elsewhere in the workflow. That extension is this Article's inference, not the survey's finding. The legally relevant requirement is therefore harness-level: the deployed process must expose the evidence and limits material to the action, trigger escalation on a tested basis, preserve a trace of how the action was assembled, and leave a qualified clinician enough information, time, authority, and institutional protection to refuse. The cascade identifies which layer generated, transformed, or suppressed the signal; the charter assigns the gate, interface, monitoring, and stop authority.
+
+Utah's Doctronic pilot makes the performance question concrete. Its regulatory-mitigation agreement originally contemplated a staged transition from prospective physician review to retrospective review and then sampled auditing of prescription renewals. Utah later tightened the transition criterion, and current state materials report that the pilot remains in Phase 1, with every request requiring authorization by a licensed medical practitioner.\lrfootnote{See \bluecite{utahDoctronicRMA2026}; \artcite{gerkeUtahRenewal2026}.} The initial validation reviewer is not, by itself, the answer to the anti-substitution inquiry. If case-specific review is later removed, the institution must identify what continues to perform the legally protected function: which conditions force escalation, what information reaches an empowered clinician, who monitors drift and exceptions, and who can suspend the system. Regulatory forbearance changes the enforcement posture. It does not itself constitute professional judgment or follow-up.
 
 Relational AI presents a different opportunity and the same legal structure. A companion can reduce isolation, translate, remind, help a user rehearse a difficult exchange, or extend supportive contact. Its availability does not authorize a care institution to withdraw staffing required by another law, an insurer to deny covered treatment, or a public program to replace an accessible service path with an endless automated loop. The relevant comparison is not human versus machine in the abstract. It is the protection promised by the governing duty versus the protection actually delivered through the deployment.
 
